# Shared declarations for every scheduled OSM extract model.
#
# One `{code}_osm_staging.preql` per city, each run by its own `osm-{code}`
# [[cloud.job]] in ../trilogy.toml — never by a city refresh. This file holds
# everything those models have in common so a city's own file is just its two
# datasources: the rows script and the staging target it writes.
#
# Deliberately standalone. Nothing here imports raw/, and nothing in raw/
# imports this, which is what keeps a city refresh job from ever adopting an
# extract asset. The concepts below mirror the canonical tree columns *by
# name* because the staging parquet's column names are the contract
# raw/{code}/{city}_tree_info.preql reads — they are not the same concepts as
# tree_common.preql's, and importing that file here would drag the raw model
# tree in behind them.
#
# The freshness watermark is Overpass's global `osm_base` timestamp, which
# advances about every minute. That is the watermark a *city* model must
# never use — it would rebuild the city on every tick, which is what made
# Boston and Tempe rebuild three times a day before staging existed — and it
# is exactly right here: every scheduled firing sees a newer timestamp and
# re-extracts, so a city's cron IS its extraction cadence. An unreachable
# Overpass degrades to the epoch (see overpass_timestamp.py), so the staging
# parquet compares fresh and the firing no-ops instead of failing.

# One script serves every city. Each city's model points its rows datasource
# at `./osm_rows.py` and adds `where city = '<CODE>'`, which Trilogy compiles
# into both a SQL predicate and a `--filter 'city=<CODE>'` argument -- that
# argument is how the script knows what to fetch. It replaced seventeen
# eight-line shims that differed only in a string.
#
# The `where` is mandatory here in a way it is not elsewhere. For
# `community_tree_info.py` a pushdown filter is an optimisation: that script
# reads a static export, and the SQL predicate applies the same restriction
# even if the argument never arrives. Here the filter decides what we *fetch*,
# so its absence would mean querying Overpass for all seventeen bounding boxes
# on every firing -- against an API that allows two concurrent slots per IP.
# `osm_rows.py` therefore refuses to run without it rather than defaulting to
# everything, and `test_every_staging_model_pushes_down_its_city` checks that
# each model supplies one.

key tree_id string;
property tree_id.city string;
property tree_id.data_source string;
property tree_id.species string;
property tree_id.tree_name string;
property tree_id.plant_date date;
property tree_id.latitude float;
property tree_id.longitude float;
property tree_id.diameter_at_breast_height float;
property tree_id.submission_photo_url string;
property tree_id.osm_ref string; # municipal inventory id where the node carries a ref tag

property <*>.osm_extracted_through datetime; # Overpass osm_base time at extraction

# City-agnostic on purpose: the timestamp is global, so every extract model
# maps this one script onto the one freshness property.
root datasource overpass_timestamp (
    data_updated_through: osm_extracted_through
)
file `./overpass_timestamp.py`;
